---
// /hosting/import/: the import guide (D00-8: no import runs in the Worker or on the Mac). Planning a
// move to GitHub, trial imports, sizes, logs and locks, commit attribution, the converters of other
// version control systems, large files before a converted history is pushed, a folder split into a
// repository, subtree merges, duplicates and ongoing mirrors kept by the person, several
// repositories at once (a POSIX sh script made in the browser by src/scripts/import-repo.ts), what to
// set after an import, and the messages that go wrong. GitHub's own tools (its importer, Enterprise
// Importer, rulesets) are explained and linked, never re-implemented (§15.2).
//
// A static page: the bulk form is shown by its script, which asks the Worker nothing (0 requests).
// Every command comes from src/lib/import-commands.ts, with placeholders in capitals. No SSH address
// is printed (it has an at sign; the site shows nothing shaped like an email address). science.css
// only; the platform's name from src/config.ts.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import {
  BULK_MAX,
  commandText,
  convertCommands,
  IMPORTER_URL,
  mirrorCommands,
  ongoingMirrorCommands,
  parseGitUrl,
  splitCommands,
  subtreeCommands,
} from "../../lib/import-commands";
import { SOURCES } from "../../lib/limits-text";

const DOCS = "https://docs.github.com/en";
const MIGRATIONS = {
  planning: `${DOCS}/migrations/overview/planning-your-migration-to-github`,
  paths: `${DOCS}/migrations/overview/migration-paths-to-github`,
  importer: `${DOCS}/migrations/importing-source-code/using-github-importer/about-github-importer`,
  commandLine: `${DOCS}/migrations/importing-source-code/using-the-command-line-to-import-source-code/adding-locally-hosted-code-to-github`,
  enterprise: `${DOCS}/migrations/using-github-enterprise-importer`,
  mannequins: `${DOCS}/migrations/using-github-enterprise-importer/completing-your-migration-with-github-enterprise-importer/reclaiming-mannequins-for-github-enterprise-importer`,
  duplicating: `${DOCS}/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/duplicating-a-repository`,
  splitting: `${DOCS}/get-started/using-git/splitting-a-subfolder-out-into-a-new-repository`,
  subtree: `${DOCS}/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges`,
  wrongUser: `${DOCS}/pull-requests/committing-changes-to-your-project/troubleshooting-commits/why-are-my-commits-linked-to-the-wrong-user`,
  rulesets: `${DOCS}/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/about-rulesets`,
  topics: `${DOCS}/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/classifying-your-repository-with-topics`,
  licence: `${DOCS}/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/licensing-a-repository`,
};
const TOOLS = {
  gitSizer: "https://github.com/github/git-sizer",
  filterRepo: "https://github.com/newren/git-filter-repo",
  gitSvn: "https://git-scm.com/docs/git-svn",
  gitP4: "https://git-scm.com/docs/git-p4",
  hgFastExport: "https://github.com/frej/fast-export",
  gitTfs: "https://github.com/git-tfs/git-tfs",
  lfsMigrate: "https://github.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/blob/main/docs/man/git-lfs-migrate.adoc",
};

// Placeholders, in capitals: your account, the new repository, the source.
const DEST = { owner: "OWNER", name: "NAME" };
const gitlab = parseGitUrl("https://gitlab.com/LAB/NAME")!;
const trial = commandText(mirrorCommands(gitlab, { owner: "OWNER", name: "NAME-trial" }));
const sizes = ["git clone --mirror https://gitlab.com/LAB/NAME NAME.git", "cd NAME.git", "git-sizer --verbose", "git count-objects -vH"];
const svn = commandText(convertCommands({ kind: "svn", source: "https://svn.example.org/repos/NAME", dest: DEST, authors: true }));
const gitSvn = commandText(convertCommands({ kind: "git-svn", source: "https://svn.example.org/repos/NAME", dest: DEST, authors: true }));
const hg = commandText(convertCommands({ kind: "hg", source: "https://hg.example.org/NAME", dest: DEST, authors: true }));
const tfvc = commandText(
  convertCommands({ kind: "tfvc", source: "https://tfs.example.org/tfs/DefaultCollection", tfvcPath: "$/Project/Main", dest: DEST }),
);
const perforce = commandText(
  convertCommands({ kind: "perforce", source: "//depot/project", p4port: "ssl:perforce.example.org:1666", dest: DEST }),
);
const withLfs = commandText(
  convertCommands({ kind: "svn", source: "https://svn.example.org/repos/NAME", dest: DEST, lfs: true }).slice(2),
);
const authorsFile = ["jdoe = Jane Doe <JANE'S ADDRESS FOR GIT>", "(one line per user of the old system)"];
const split = commandText(splitCommands(parseGitUrl("https://github.com/OWNER/BIG-PROJECT")!, "analysis/eeg", DEST));
const subtree = commandText(subtreeCommands(parseGitUrl("https://gitlab.com/LAB/TOOLBOX")!, "vendor/toolbox"));
const ongoing = commandText(ongoingMirrorCommands(DEST));
const after = [
  'gh repo edit OWNER/NAME --description "What the code does" --homepage "https://doi.org/10.XXXX/YYYY"',
  "gh repo edit OWNER/NAME --add-topic neuroscience --add-topic eeg",
];
---
<Base
  title="Importing a repository"
  description="Planning an import, from GitLab, Subversion, Mercurial and others, keeping commit ids."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Hosting code", href: "/hosting/" }, { text: "Importing a repository" }]}
>
  <h1>Importing a repository</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    How to move a repository from another host or version control system to your GitHub account, keeping its commit
    ids whenever it is already Git. Every import runs on your own computer: {SITE_NAME} runs none and sees none of your
    credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    For one Git repository, <a href="/new/import/">the import page</a> writes the commands for you. This guide is for
    the larger moves: several repositories, another version control system, a folder that becomes a repository.
  </p>

  <h2 id="planning">Planning an import</h2>
  <p>
    Before moving anything, list what you have: the repositories, their sizes, which use Git LFS, which have submodules,
    and who pushes to them. GitHub's <a href={MIGRATIONS.planning}>planning guide</a> and its
    <a href={MIGRATIONS.paths}>migration paths</a> say which tool fits which source, and at which level: the code and
    its history only (what this guide does), or also issues, pull requests and wikis (GitHub Enterprise Importer, for
    organizations).
  </p>
  <h3 id="trial">Trial imports</h3>
  <p>
    Import once into a throwaway repository (NAME-trial), check it, delete it, then import for real. A trial costs
    nothing and shows the size, the time it takes, and any file GitHub refuses:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the trial import">{trial}</pre>
  <h3 id="sizes">Sizes, before you push</h3>
  <p>
    GitHub refuses a file over its size limit and a push over its push limit (<a href="/hosting/limits/">the limits</a>).
    <a href={TOOLS.gitSizer}>git-sizer</a> lists the largest blobs, trees and histories of a repository:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the size commands">{commandText(sizes)}</pre>
  <p>
    A push over the limit: push in steps, one range of commits at a time, as GitHub's
    <a href={SOURCES.pushLimit}>troubleshooting the 2 GiB push limit</a> shows.
  </p>
  <h3 id="logs">Migration logs, locks and aborting</h3>
  <p>
    A command-line import keeps its own log: the terminal's output. Keep it (<code>script import.log</code> before
    starting records everything). <a href={IMPORTER_URL}>GitHub's importer</a> works on its own servers: it copies one
    repository over https, does not move Git LFS objects, tells you by a notification when it has finished, and can be
    cancelled from its page while it runs (<a href={MIGRATIONS.importer}>about GitHub's importer</a>). GitHub
    Enterprise Importer can lock the source repositories during a move; a command-line import locks nothing: tell the
    people who push to stop until the new repository is in place.
  </p>

  <h2 id="attribution">Commit attribution</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub links a commit to an account by the address written in the commit, if that account has it among its own
    addresses (<a href={MIGRATIONS.wrongUser}>why commits are linked to the wrong user</a>). An import keeps the commits
    exactly as they were, addresses included: {SITE_NAME} never reads them and never shows them. Commits whose address
    no account claims show the name only. Organizations that move with GitHub Enterprise Importer get placeholder
    accounts, called mannequins, for the people it could not match, which they
    <a href={MIGRATIONS.mannequins}>reclaim afterwards</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gitlab">From GitLab, Bitbucket, Codeberg or a lab's server</h2>
  <p>
    Any Git host is imported the same way: git clone --mirror, then git push --mirror into an empty repository, every
    branch, tag and commit id kept. <a href="/new/import/">The import page</a> writes these commands for your addresses;
    GitHub's <a href={MIGRATIONS.commandLine}>command-line import</a> explains them. The source's merge and pull request
    refs stay behind: GitHub refuses them on a push.
  </p>

  <h2 id="other-systems">From another version control system</h2>
  <p>
    Converting from another system writes a new Git history: the commits get new ids. Tracing maps made on the old
    system cannot carry over; maps made after the import can. Each converter runs on your computer. In the commands,
    authors.txt maps the old system's user names to the names Git writes; you write that file yourself, one line per
    user:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands">{commandText(authorsFile)}</pre>
  <h3 id="subversion">Subversion</h3>
  <p>With svn2git, for a repository with the usual trunk, branches and tags:</p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the Subversion commands (svn2git)">{svn}</pre>
  <p>Or with Git's own <a href={TOOLS.gitSvn}>git svn</a>:</p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the Subversion commands (git svn)">{gitSvn}</pre>
  <h3 id="mercurial">Mercurial</h3>
  <p>With <a href={TOOLS.hgFastExport}>hg-fast-export</a>:</p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the Mercurial commands">{hg}</pre>
  <h3 id="tfvc">TFVC</h3>
  <p>With <a href={TOOLS.gitTfs}>git-tfs</a> (Team Foundation Version Control):</p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the TFVC commands">{tfvc}</pre>
  <h3 id="perforce">Perforce</h3>
  <p>With Git's own <a href={TOOLS.gitP4}>git p4</a>:</p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the Perforce commands">{perforce}</pre>
  <h3 id="large-files">Large files before the first push</h3>
  <p>
    A converted history is new, so rewriting it costs nothing: move the large files into Git LFS before the first push
    (<a href={TOOLS.lfsMigrate}>git lfs migrate</a>), or GitHub refuses the push. After the conversion, in its folder:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the LFS commands">{withLfs}</pre>
  <p><a href="/hosting/large-files/">Large files</a> says where data belongs before it goes into LFS.</p>

  <h2 id="split">A folder of a repository into a new repository</h2>
  <p>
    With <a href={TOOLS.filterRepo}>git filter-repo</a>, the folder becomes the new repository's root, with the history
    of its files only (<a href={MIGRATIONS.splitting}>splitting a subfolder</a>). These commits get new ids.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the split commands">{split}</pre>

  <h2 id="subtree">Another repository into a folder of yours: a subtree merge</h2>
  <p>
    Inside the receiving repository, the other one's history is merged under a folder, and its new commits can be
    brought in later (<a href={MIGRATIONS.subtree}>about Git subtree merges</a>):
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the subtree commands">{subtree}</pre>

  <h2 id="duplicate">A copy without a fork, and an ongoing mirror</h2>
  <p>
    A fork stays attached to its source on GitHub; a duplicate is a repository of its own, every commit id kept
    (<a href={MIGRATIONS.duplicating}>duplicating a repository</a>): it is the import above, from a GitHub address. When
    the source goes on living elsewhere, you can keep your copy up to date yourself, from the folder of the first
    import, by hand or from your own scheduler. {SITE_NAME} runs no mirror for you.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the update commands">{ongoing}</pre>

  <h2 id="bulk">Several repositories at once</h2>
  <p>
    List the repositories, one a line: the source's git address, then, if you like, the new name (or account/name) and
    the word lfs when the source uses Git LFS. The page writes a shell script (POSIX sh) that imports them one after
    the other, each in its own block, stopping at the first error. It is made in your browser and sent nowhere; it holds
    no credential: git asks GitHub for yours. Create each repository first, empty. At most {BULK_MAX} repositories in one
    script.
  </p>
  <form id="bulk-form" hidden>
    <p>
      <label for="bulk-owner">Your GitHub account</label><br />
      <input type="text" id="bulk-owner" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" maxlength="39" />
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="bulk-list">The repositories, one a line</label><br />
      <textarea id="bulk-list" rows="6" cols="60" spellcheck="false" placeholder="https://gitlab.com/lab/eeg-analysis.git eeg-analysis lfs"></textarea>
    </p>
    <p><button type="submit">Write the script</button></p>
    <p id="bulk-message" role="status"></p>
    <ul id="bulk-errors" hidden></ul>
  </form>
  <section id="bulk-result" class="setup" hidden>
    <h3>The script</h3>
    <p>Run it in an empty folder: sh import.sh</p>
    <pre class="commands" id="bulk-script" data-copy="the script"></pre>
    <p id="bulk-download"></p>
  </section>
  <noscript><p>Without JavaScript, run the commands of <a href="/new/import/">the import page</a> once per repository.</p></noscript>

  <h2 id="after">After the import</h2>
  <p>
    An import brings the history, not the repository's settings: set its description, website and
    <a href={MIGRATIONS.topics}>topics</a> on GitHub (or with GitHub's command-line tool, gh), and check that it has a
    <a href={MIGRATIONS.licence}>licence</a>: without one, {SITE_NAME} keeps no copy of its scripts. Then
    <a href="/new/link/">link it</a> to its papers.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the settings commands">{commandText(after)}</pre>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">When it goes wrong</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>remote: error: File … is … MB; this exceeds GitHub's file size limit</dt>
    <dd>A file over the limit is in the history: move it into Git LFS before pushing (above), or leave it out.</dd>
    <dt>fatal: the remote end hung up unexpectedly, or a push that stops</dt>
    <dd>Often a push over the push limit: push in steps (<a href={SOURCES.pushLimit}>the 2 GiB push limit</a>).</dd>
    <dt>! [remote rejected] refs/pull/… (deny updating a hidden ref)</dt>
    <dd>The source's pull request refs: the mirror commands leave them behind; delete them as the commands do.</dd>
    <dt>! [remote rejected] … (push declined due to repository rule violations)</dt>
    <dd>
      A ruleset of the destination refuses the push. Import into a repository without rulesets, then add them; bypass
      lists are set by the repository's admins (<a href={MIGRATIONS.rulesets}>about rulesets</a>).
    </dd>
    <dt>Authentication failed, or a password prompt that refuses your password</dt>
    <dd>GitHub takes a token as the password over https: <a href="/hosting/tokens/">tokens for git</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="enterprise">Organizations and GitHub Enterprise</h2>
  <p>
    Moving an organization's repositories with their issues and pull requests is
    <a href={MIGRATIONS.enterprise}>GitHub Enterprise Importer</a>'s work, and live migrations between Enterprise
    servers are GitHub's own service: {SITE_NAME} does not re-implement them. Once the repositories are on GitHub,
    link them here like any other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="more">More</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/new/import/">Import a repository</a>: the commands for one.</li>
    <li><a href="/hosting/leave/">Leaving the registry</a>: your repository stays yours.</li>
    <li><a href="/hosting/">The hosting guides</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <script>
    import "../../scripts/import-repo";
  </script>
</Base>
